Important note
==============

Skia packaged in Debian (libskia) does not receive security support
during the life time of the stable release. It should therefore not be
used by security-sensitive applications that process potentially
untrusted data (e.g. web browsers).

There are two main reasons it is not feasible to provide security
support for libskia:

1. Major versions (which correspond to chrome milestones) are released
   frequently, every two weeks at the time of writing. LTS branches
   exist, but are only maintained for a few of months. While the API
   is mostly stable across releases, API changes do happen, which
   makes it necessary to backport changes to stable rather than
   upgrading to a new major version.
   
2. Since Skia is used in Chromium, there is a steady stream of CVEs.
   While fixes are often small, internal changes in Skia between major
   versions (e.g. transitioning to a new renderer) makes the changes
   increasingly difficult to backport patches as the difference
   between the version in stable and upstream's version increase.

The two points above also mean that security-sensitive applications
(e.g. web browsers) should probably embed a copy of libskia anyway.
The most up-to-date of the application will probably target the latest
version of Skia, which may have small API incompatibilities compared
to the version in Debian.

Skia (and particularly pathops) is still useful in Debian. For
example, various fonts in Debian rely on pathops from Skia as a part
of their build process.
